  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA low pressure system approaches from the west this morning, with a \nwarm front lifting into the region and scattered showers developing \nahead of it. Shower coverage will increase through the day as the \nassociated cold front approaches by mid afternoon. Model soundings \ncontinue to show PWAT values approaching 2 inches at times, \nsupporting the potential for locally heavy downpours. \n\nInstability remains modest, with CAPE generally a few hundred J/Kg \nearly in the day and potentially increasing to around 500-600J/Kg by \nafternoon according to the NAM. However, ongoing morning showers and \ncloud cover may limit destabilization, so the degree of afternoon \ninstability remains somewhat uncertain. Deep layer shear of around \n30 knots will be present, though low and mid level lapse rates \nremain rather weak. Given these parameters, any isolated severe \nthunderstorm potential should be mainly confined to the Southern \nTier of NY into Northeast PA. Sounding profiles also show long \nskinny CAPE, favoring efficient rainfall production. A marginal risk \nfor excessive rainfall remains in place across the western portion \nof the region, where isolated flash flooding will be possible. \n\nShowers will gradually end from west to east Thursday evening, as \nthe front moves east of the area. Temperatures ahead of the front \nwill rise into the mid 70s to near 80 possible where greater \nbreaks in the clouds and showers occur. Rainfall amounts today \nare generally expected to range from one third to half an inch, \nwith locally higher amounts possible in thunderstorms.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nHigh pressure Will build into the region behind the front, \nbringing a cooler and drier air mass for Friday. Mostly sunny \nskies are expected through the day, with mainly clear conditions\ncontinuing into early Saturday morning. Under the influence of \nthe cooler air mass and surface high, temperatures Friday and \nSaturday will generally only reach the mid 60s to lower 70s. The\ncooler air will be more noticeable overnight, with lows falling\ninto the lower 40s to lower 50s. Some of the typically cooler \nlocations across northern Oneida county could dip into the upper\n30s. Clouds will then begin to increase Saturday afternoon \nahead of the next approaching system.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... \n\nAnother low pressure system will approach from the west \nSaturday night into Sunday, bringing chances for showers. \nSouthwesterly flow ahead of the low will transport moisture \nnorthward, supporting the potential for moderate to widespread \nrainfall. Guidance has trended a bit slower with the passage of \nthe low compared to yesterday, likely due to lingering influence\nfrom the surface high earlier in the weekend. Showers may \ntherefore continue through much of Sunday before gradually \ntapering off by Sunday evening. Monday currently looks mainly \ndry as surface high pressure develops north of the Great Lakes. \nModel guidance begins to diverge by Tuesday, leading to \nincreasing uncertainity in how the midweek pattern will evolve.",
